Hello,

I still am surprised at my FIRST ascension (with a wizard), but I've
managed to achieve my second with an archaeologist. I random-genned
the Lawful alignment which was probably fortuitous for the easy access
to Grayswandir, though it's not an alignment I'm used to playing, so
that was interesting (couple times accidentally made myself "guilty"
managed to stay in good enough graces with my god).

I'd been playing archaeologists a lot (intermixed with other classes)
but was having a weird mix of the impatience curse and very, very odd
luck (such as finding artifacts in stores, but no altars or scrolls of
identify).

I decided to try an archaelogist "one more time" and the RNG
apparently repented. They put an aligned altar on nearly every level
for the first several levels in.... and one in Minetown. I actually
assumed this was a sign I was going to die quickly for overconfidence,
but I tried to play very slowly and carefully so as not to fall into
that trap. I did get Grayswandir very early, though, and that was
enormously helpful, since my biggest source of frustration with
playing archs before was not finding any good weapons beyond my slow-
swinging pickaxe for a very long time (I tried on one occasion to use
a sling and worthless pieces of glass, but I was getting weighed down
with ammo or losing it too quickly).

But in a way, the real saving grace of all those altars was an added
presence of a fair number of fountains and potions I didn't need, so I
got myself a lot of holy water, which is a more amazing boon than I
first realized.

My other issue playing archs--getting my AC down--was helped by
finding an armor shop and a blessed scroll of ID; the shop contained
+1 studded leather, water walking boots, a helm of brilliance, and an
alchemy smock, which were quickly put to good use. The smock later got
replaced by a robe (no other magic cloaks showed up in the game except
for a cloak of protection much later on, and I preferred the robe).

Unusually well-equipped, I went into minetown for gems and the
luckstone. Took my time and tried to hold onto my pets, as I was still
slightly fragile for awhile (a winged gargoyle in Minetown nearly
killed me. Thank goodness for the aligned temple!). I found a bag of
holding and a magic whistle (one of those "minor" magic items that
seldom get discussed, but I often find myself longing for if I don't
have one) down there; I think the BoH was in an otherwise not very
useful Bones file. Somewhere I found a ring of slow digestion and a
ring of free action which were my primary rings for most of the game
(there were also 2 other randomly found rings of free action later).

Sokoban netted me a amulet of reflection. I found a ton of spellbooks
in the game, and got my attack, enchantment, and divination abilities
up as high as they could go. Magic Missile, extra healing, and detect
monsters, and clairvoyance got cast a lot; much later I found a +of
Charm Monster which got me some useful pets, including a Blue Dragon
who helped me out for a long time before being killed by my Quest
nemesis.

I did Medusa and the Castle before the quest. Perseus's statue didn't
have the Shield of Reflection, and of course I abused my
archaeologistness for destroying it. Castle wand wishes included a
spellbook of identify and the only magic marker I had in the game,
plus some scrolls of charging and some gray dragon scale mail (I was
tempted to do silver, and then wish for a cloak of magic resistance,
but that was an additional wish when I also needed some other stuff).
Later on, I spent the rest of the wand, plus a wish granted by a
throne, on boots of speed and gauntlets of power (I wished for these
very late in the game, and of course later I found more boots of speed
and gloves of dexterity, when no such things had been found
previously. ah well.). Some of the scrolls of charging didn't charge
up my magic marker like I wanted them to, and I prolly should have
just wished for more magic markers instead.

Quest wasn't too hard, but I think that's because I waited till I had
a quite low AC and good weapon skills and spells. Orb of Detection was
somewhat useful, but as artifacts go I have to say it's not too
exciting.

Major near-death experience in Gehennom when encountering my first and
last master mind flayer. "I'm high level," I think, "I've got massive
whupass skills. He should be no problem!" He gets closer to me sooner
than I think he's able to, and one narrow escape later with a confused
scroll of teleport (and then having to re-identify half my inventory
and re-map half the levels) I'm genociding all h's. BTW, game gave me
four scrolls of genocide. I blessed and used two, and never got around
to the other two.

Ran around Gehennom for about 800 years looking for the stairs to
Vlad's Tower. On the other hand, the vibrating square was easily
found, so I guess that's a tradeoff. Other demons encountered fairly
typical: Asmodeus, Baalzebub, Juiblex, Orcus. Orcus spent his wand up
and I couldn't get it to recharge. No death spells the whole game.

Bottom level wasn't too hard between good AC and Charm Monster (half
the time I cast it just to keep from being surrounded). Made sure the
amulet couldn't be confined in such trappings and named it Real.

Dashed upstairs; didn't get pulled back downstairs too many times--is
that a side effect of being Lawful? Rodney only attacked me a handful
of times, though he double troubled me once, which was annoying.

On my way upstairs, I found a peaceful Archon wandering around. I
decided to try casting Charm Monster on him, and lo, I have a pet who
comes with Demonbane and a Silver Shield. He was definitely helpful on
the run through the elemental planes (and still alive when I ascended,
despite several air elementals' attempts on his life).

Orb of Detection helped me find portals in Elemental planes. Pretty
much dashed through all the elements as fast as I could. Air
Elementals hurt me a bit, but wands of fire seem to help kill them
pretty quickly.

On the astral plane, I almost try right first, but see Death behind a
locked door, and decide to try elsewhere first. I equip a ring of
conflict which I didn't find till quite close to the ascension run.
Center altar is a bust. But going left, past a swiftly killed Famine
and Pestilence, is my lawful altar, and while the priests and angels
are beating up on each other, I become a demigoddess...

